    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Execute the rigidity experiment pipeline by running the Python script that sweeps hidden layer widths (N) and sample counts (w), trains small feedforward networks on the Barron-class function, and records N_c(w) values where validation error ≤ 1e-3. The workflow includes data generation, convergence checking (ensuring finite N_c), and scaling analysis to test whether N_c remains constant (rigid) or grows (floppy)."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The experiment will produce a table of N_c(w) values across increasing w. If the Structure (Approximation Theory) hypothesis holds, N_c will plateau at a small O(1) value (e.g., N_c ∈ [5, 15]) for all tested w. If the Geometry or Measure hypotheses dominate, N_c will show linear or fractional power-law growth with w. All N_c values must be finite integers."
    },
